RADIUS - Protocol vs Server
RADIUS = the protocol. It's the rules for how the messages are formatted. Like HTTP is a protocol.
A RADIUS server = a machine running software that speaks RADIUS (like FreeRADIUS or Windows NPS). Like Apache is a server that speaks HTTP.
You don't "install RADIUS." You install FreeRADIUS or enable NPS on a Windows box. That makes it a RADIUS server.

RADIUS is the language Mutley radios in. The RADIUS server is the manager who speaks that language. Mutley doesn't radio in "manager-speak" — he radios in RADIUS. 
The manager just happens to be running software that understands RADIUS.

That's the whole difference. Protocol = the language. Server = the person speaking it.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
What is RADIUS Protocol
RADIUS — What It Actually Is
RADIUS = Remote Authentication Dial-In User Service 

It's a protocol — a set of rules for how messages get sent between two computers.  Specifically, it's the language that Mutley (the switch/bouncer) uses to radio
the manager in the back (the RADIUS server) to ask "is this guy allowed in?"

The Three Jobs It Does (AAA)
Job	Question it answers	In your story
Authentication	- "Is Allen actually Allen?"	Manager checks the password. Yes/No.
Authorization  - 	"Okay, Allen's real. What can he do?"	Manager says "he gets the VIP lounge, not the basement."
Accounting	-    "What did Allen do while he was in here?"	Manager writes down: "Allen was in from 9 to 5, used 4GB of data."

How the Messages Flow
Allen plugs in (or connects to Wi-Fi).
Mutley (the switch) gets blocked — port is locked.
Mutley sends an Access-Request packet to the RADIUS server: "This guy Allen says his password is XYZ. Is it right?"
RADIUS server checks its database.  Three possible answers:
Access-Accept → "Yeah, let him in. And put him on VLAN 10."
Access-Reject → "Nope. Not on the list."
Access-Challenge → "I need more info.  Ask him for his token code."
Mutley gets the answer, opens or keeps the door shut.
If accepted, Mutley sends Accounting-Start to the server. When Allen leaves, Accounting-Stop.

The Boring Exam Details
Detail	Value
                  Transport	UDP (not TCP)
                  Auth/Authorization port	1812
                  Accounting port	1813
                  Old/legacy ports	1645 / 1646
Security	Only the password field is encrypted (using a shared secret).  The rest of the packet is basically cleartext.
Standard	IETF RFC 2865 (auth), RFC 2866 (accounting)

The Key Confusion on the Exam
The RADIUS client is NOT Allen. The RADIUS client is Mutley (the switch/NAS). Allen is the supplicant. Mutley is the one talking RADIUS to the server.
Allen just talks EAP to Mutley. Mutley then wraps that in RADIUS and sends it to the server.

So: Allen speaks EAP to Mutley. Mutley speaks RADIUS to the server.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
What is Radius Server?
RADIUS = Remote Authentication Dial-In User Service. 

Analogy = Radius is the manager in the back office. The one Mutley calls in on the radios when Allen shows up at the door.

Here's the deal:

Mutley - (the switch/switch port) doesn't know Allen's password. He doesn't have the list. He's just a bouncer dog — he holds the door,
nothing more.
RADIUS is the server sitting somewhere on the network that actually has the list of who's allowed in and what their passwords are. 
When Mutley radios in "this fool Allen wants in," RADIUS checks its database (which might be Active Directory, or LDAP, or just a flat file) and says "Access-Accept" or "Access-Reject".
Three things RADIUS does (AAA):

Function	In your story
Authentication	"Is Allen actually Allen? Check his password."
Authorization	"Okay, Allen's real. Which room can he go in? The VIP lounge or the basement?"
Accounting	"Allen was in from 9am to 5pm. Write that down."

Exam stuff to remember:

Runs over UDP port 1812 (auth) and 1813 (accounting)
It's a client-server protocol — but the "client" is Mutley (the switch/NAS), NOT Allen.  This trips people up on the exam.
It was made in 1991 for dial-up, but now it's used for Wi-Fi, VPNs, wired ports — everything. 
Common RADIUS server software: FreeRADIUS (Linux), NPS (Windows — Network Policy Server)
So the full chain: Allen → Mutley → RADIUS → decision → back to Mutley → door opens or stays shut.

